() Tj T*
( _In dark and early ages, through the primal forests faring,) Tj T*
( Ere the soul came shining into prehistoric night,) Tj T*
( Twofold man was equal; they were comrades dear and daring,) Tj T*
( Living wild and free together in unreasoning delight._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
( _Ere the soul was born and consciousness came slowly,) Tj T*
( Ere the soul was born, to man and woman, too,) Tj T*
( Ere he found the Tree of Knowledge, that awful tree and holy,) Tj T*
( Ere he knew he felt, and knew he knew._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
( _Then said he to Pain, ?I am wise now, and I know you!) Tj T*
( No more will I suffer while power and wisdom last!?) Tj T*
( Then said he to Pleasure, ?I am strong, and I will show you) Tj T*
( That the will of man can seize you,?aye, and hold you fast!?_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
( _Food he ate for pleasure, and wine he drank for gladness.) Tj T*
( And woman? Ah, the woman! the crown of all delight!) Tj T*
( His now,?he knew it! He was strong to madness) Tj T*
( In that early dawning after prehistoric night._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
( _His,?his forever! That glory sweet and tender!) Tj T*
( Ah, but he would love her! And she should love but him!) Tj T*
( He would work and struggle for her, he would shelter and defend her,?) Tj T*
( She should never leave him, never, till their eyes in death were dim._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
( _Close, close he bound her, that she should leave him never;) Tj T*
( Weak still he kept her, lest she be strong to flee;) Tj T*
( And the fainting flame of passion he kept alive forever) Tj T*
( With all the arts and forces of earth and sky and sea._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
( _And, ah, the long journey! The slow and awful ages) Tj T*
( They have labored up together, blind and crippled, all astray!) Tj T*
( Through what a mighty volume, with a million shameful pages,) Tj T*
ET
