( From the freedom of the forests to the prisons of to-day!_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
( _Food he ate for pleasure, and it slew him with diseases!) Tj T*
( Wine he drank for gladness, and it led the way to crime!) Tj T*
( And woman? He will hold her,?he will have her when he pleases,?) Tj T*
( And he never once hath seen her since the prehistoric time!_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
( _Gone the friend and comrade of the day when life was younger,) Tj T*
( She who rests and comforts, she who helps and saves.) Tj T*
( Still he seeks her vainly, with a never-dying hunger;) Tj T*
( Alone beneath his tyrants, alone above his slaves!_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
( _Toiler, bent and weary with the load of thine own making!) Tj T*
( Thou who art sad and lonely, though lonely all in vain!) Tj T*
( Who hast sought to conquer Pleasure and have her for the taking,) Tj T*
( And found that Pleasure only was another name for Pain_?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
( _Nature hath reclaimed thee, forgiving dispossession!) Tj T*
( God hath not forgotten, though man doth still forget!) Tj T*
( The woman-soul is rising, in despite of thy transgression?) Tj T*
( Loose her now, and trust her! She will love thee yet!_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
( _Love thee? She will love thee as only freedom knoweth!) Tj T*
( Love thee? She will love thee while Love itself doth live!) Tj T*
( Fear not the heart of woman! No bitterness it showeth!) Tj T*
( The ages of her sorrow have but taught her to forgive!_) Tj T*
