(resist natural law is well proven by the life and death of the ascetic.) Tj T*
(In any one of those suicidal martyrs may be seen the will, misdirected) Tj T*
(by the ill-informed intelligence, forcing the body to defy every natural) Tj T*
(impulse,?even to the door of death, and through it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But, while these exceptions show what the human will can do, the general) Tj T*
(course of life shows the inexorable effect of conditions upon humanity.) Tj T*
(Of these conditions we share with other living things the environment of) Tj T*
(the material universe. We are affected by climate and locality, by) Tj T*
(physical, chemical, electrical forces, as are all animals and plants.) Tj T*
(With the animals, we farther share the effect of our own activity, the) Tj T*
(reactionary force of exercise. What we do, as well as what is done to) Tj T*
(us, makes us what we are. But, beyond these forces, we come under the) Tj T*
(effect of a third set of conditions peculiar to our human status;) Tj T*
(namely, social conditions. In the organic interchanges which constitute) Tj T*
(social life, we are affected by each other to a degree beyond what is) Tj T*
(found even among the most gregarious of animals. This third factor, the) Tj T*
(social environment, is of enormous force as a modifier of human life.) Tj T*
(Throughout all these environing conditions, those which affect us) Tj T*
(through our economic necessities are most marked in their influence.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Without touching yet upon the influence of the social factors, treating) Tj T*
(the human being merely as an individual animal, we see that he is) Tj T*
(modified most by his economic conditions, as is every other animal.) Tj T*
(Differ as they may in color and size, in strength and speed, in minor) Tj T*
(adaptation to minor conditions, all animals that live on grass have) Tj T*
(distinctive traits in common, and all animals that eat flesh have) Tj T*
(distinctive traits in common,?so distinctive and so common that it is by) Tj T*
(teeth, by nutritive apparatus in general, that they are classified,) Tj T*
(rather than by means of defence or locomotion. The food supply of the) Tj T*
(animal is the largest passive factor in his development; the processes) Tj T*
(by which he obtains his food supply, the largest active factor in his) Tj T*
(development. It is these activities, the incessant repetition of the) Tj T*
(exertions by which he is fed, which most modify his structure and) Tj T*
(develope his functions. The sheep, the cow, the deer, differ in their) Tj T*
(adaptation to the weather, their locomotive ability, their means of) Tj T*
(defence; but they agree in main characteristics, because of their common) Tj T*
(method of nutrition.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The human animal is no exception to this rule. Climate affects him,) Tj T*
(weather affects him, enemies affect him; but most of all he is affected,) Tj T*
(like every other living creature, by what he does for his living. Under) Tj T*
(all the influence of his later and wider life, all the reactive effect) Tj T*
(of social institutions, the individual is still inexorably modified by) Tj T*
(his means of livelihood: ?the hand of the dyer is subdued to what he) Tj T*
(works in.? As one clear, world-known instance of the effect of economic) Tj T*
(conditions upon the human creature, note the marked race-modification of) Tj T*
(the Hebrew people under the enforced restrictions of the last two) Tj T*
(thousand years. Here is a people rising to national prominence, first as) Tj T*
(a pastoral, and then as an agricultural nation; only partially) Tj T*
(commercial through race affinity with the Ph?nicians, the pioneer) Tj T*
(traders of the world. Under the social power of a united) Tj T*
