() Tj T*
(It is not alone upon woman, and, through her, upon the race, that the) Tj T*
(ill-effects may be observed. Man, as master, has suffered from his) Tj T*
(position also. The lust for power and conquest, natural to the male of) Tj T*
(any species, has been fostered in him to an enormous degree by this) Tj T*
(cheap and easy lordship. His dominance is not that of one chosen as best) Tj T*
(fitted to rule or of one ruling by successful competition with ?foemen) Tj T*
(worthy of his steel?; but it is a sovereignty based on the accident of) Tj T*
(sex, and holding over such helpless and inferior dependants as could not) Tj T*
(question or oppose. The easy superiority that needs no striving to) Tj T*
(maintain it; the temptation to cruelty always begotten by irresponsible) Tj T*
(power; the pride and self-will which surely accompany it,?these) Tj T*
(qualities have been bred into the souls of men by their side of the) Tj T*
(relation. When man?s place was maintained by brute force, it made him) Tj T*
(more brutal: when his place was maintained by purchase, by the power of) Tj T*
(economic necessity, then he grew into the merciless use of such power as) Tj T*
(distinguishes him to-day.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Another giant evil engendered by this relation is what we call) Tj T*
(selfishness. Social life tends to reduce this feeling, which is but a) Tj T*
(belated individualism; but the sexuo-economic relation fosters and) Tj T*
(developes it. To have a whole human creature consecrated to his direct) Tj T*
(personal service, to pleasing and satisfying him in every way) Tj T*
(possible,?this has kept man selfish beyond the degree incidental to our) Tj T*
(stage of social growth. Even in our artificial society life men are more) Tj T*
(forbearing and considerate, more polite and kind, than they are at home.) Tj T*
(Pride, cruelty, and selfishness are the vices of the master; and these) Tj T*
(have been kept strong in the bosom of the family through the false) Tj T*
(position of woman. And every human soul is born, an impressionable) Tj T*
(child, into the close presence of these conditions. Our men must live in) Tj T*
(the ethics of a civilized, free, industrial, democratic age; but they) Tj T*
(are born and trained in the moral atmosphere of a primitive) Tj T*
(patriarchate. No wonder that we are all somewhat slow to rise to the) Tj T*
(full powers and privileges of democracy, to feel full social honor and) Tj T*
(social duty, while every soul of us is reared in this stronghold of) Tj T*
(ancient and outgrown emotions,?the economically related family.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(So we may trace from the sexuo-economic relation of our species not only) Tj T*
(definite evils in psychic development, bred severally in men and women,) Tj T*
(and transmitted indifferently to their offspring, but the innate) Tj T*
(perversion of character resultant from the moral miscegenation of two so) Tj T*
(diverse souls,?the unfailing shadow and distortion which has darkened) Tj T*
(and twisted the spirit of man from its beginnings. We have been injured) Tj T*
(in body and in mind by the too dissimilar traits inherited from our) Tj T*
(widely separated parents, but nowhere is the injury more apparent than) Tj T*
(in its ill effects upon the moral nature of the race.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Yet here, as in the other evil results of the sexuo-economic relation,) Tj T*
(we can see the accompanying good that made the condition necessary in) Tj T*
(its time; and we can follow the beautiful results of our present changes) Tj T*
(with comforting assurance. A healthy, normal moral sense will be ours,) Tj T*
(freed from its exaggerations and contradictions; and, with that clear) Tj T*
ET
